{:nav-title "Input params"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Input Parameters"]
  [:p "Every query so far has had its filter values written directly into the query. "
   "The " [:code ":in"] " clause lets you declare named inputs, so the same query "
   "can be run with different values, exactly like function parameters."]
  [:p "Every one of those earlier queries actually had an implicit " [:code ":in $"] ": the database itself, "
   "conventionally named " [:code "$"] ". When you write " [:code ":in"] " yourself, you list " [:code "$"]
   " explicitly, followed by your own inputs, in the same order the query expects them. "
   "The site passes " [:code "$"] " for you either way. "
   "In Clojure you would call a query with one extra input like this:"]
  [:ui/code "clojure"
   "(d/q '[:find ?name
       :in   $ ?type
       :where [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
              [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]
     db \"Fire\")"]
  [:p "In the editors below, the inputs are written after the query, one per line, "
   "in the same order as " [:code ":in"] " (after " [:code "$"] "). You can edit them and run the query again."]

  [:h2 "Single scalar"]
  [:p "A bare variable in " [:code ":in"] " binds a single value. "
   "This is the most common form. Here " [:code "?type"] " is bound to " [:code "\"Fire\""] ":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :in $ ?type
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]
   "Fire"]
  [:p "You can declare as many inputs as you need. Pass one value for each, in order. "
   "If a value is missing, Datomic tells you how many inputs it expected. "
   "That count includes " [:code "$"] ", so a query with one input of its own expects 2."]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?speed
    :in $ ?type ?min-speed
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(>= ?speed ?min-speed)]]
   "Fire"
   100]

  [:h2 "Collection binding"]
  [:p "Wrap the variable in " [:code "[?var ...]"] " to accept "
   "a collection of values. The query matches any entity whose attribute value "
   "appears in the collection, like SQL's " [:code "IN (...)"] ":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?type
    :in $ [?type ...]
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]
   ["Ghost" "Dragon"]]

  [:h2 "Tuple binding"]
  [:p "Wrap multiple variables in " [:code "[?a ?b]"] " to destructure "
   "a single input tuple. Useful for passing a pair of related values, like a range:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?speed
    :in $ [?min-speed ?max-speed]
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(>= ?speed ?min-speed)]
    [(<= ?speed ?max-speed)]]
   [80 100]]

  [:h2 "Relation binding"]
  [:p "A relation binding accepts a collection of tuples, like a small in-memory table. "
   "Each tuple is destructured into the declared variables. "
   "Here every row pairs a Pokemon's name with the least HP we want to see for it:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?hp
    :in $ [[?name ?min-hp]]
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/hp ?hp]
    [(>= ?hp ?min-hp)]]
   [["Pikachu" 30]
    ["Charizard" 70]]]
  [:p "Each row in the input relation acts like a separate filter applied in parallel, "
   "then the results are combined."]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Change the range in the tuple example to " [:code "[50 70]"] " to see which Pokemon fall in that band. "
    "Or add another type, like " [:code "\"Fire\""] ", to the collection example and run it again."]]]}
